# Sema

Name resolution, type checking, the semantic checks, and the lowerings that
reduce a checked tree to the core Kairo Codegen emits. Complete through name
and type resolution (`--type-check-only` is the dependable loop); lowering
and codegen are in progress.

The specs, read before changing anything here:
- RESOLUTION.md  THE spec: pass order (P -> I -> E -> V -> N -> T ->
                 ChainBinding -> checks), what each pass owns, the hard
                 invariants (§3), and what remains (§4).
- IMPORTS.md     the import / overlay model, module roots and paths.
- ../Codegen/CODEGEN.md  the contract between Lower/ and Codegen.

Layout:
- Sema.k, SemaContext.k, SemaDump.k  driver, shared state, --print-sema
- Resolve/  name, member, type and import resolution, redecl merge, chain
            binding, access checks
- Type/     ExprTyper and its parts: calls, overload resolution, argument
            deduction, conversions, casts, literals, operators, patterns
- Check/    semantic checks: class lifecycle, conformance, init / move /
            const, control flow, returns, patterns, effects, layout
- Verify/   structural checks after resolution (arm order, labels, loop
            context, terminators, C++ names, module pins)
- Expand/   macro expansion, eval, requires desugaring
- Foreign/  C++ (ffi) decls in and out: header index, decl/type import,
            foreign instantiation
- Mono/     monomorphization, witness tables
- Lower/    one pass per construct lowered away (match, nullable, panic,
            finally, operators, iteration, yield, ...)

## Done (kept here so they are not re-filed)

- Import folding: imports become scoped forward declarations and callers
  are rewritten to the right target (`alloc(1024)` -> `mem::alloc(1024)`).
  Resolve/ImportResolution.k with AST/Context/ImportOverlay.k; this is what
  lets the rest of Sema run per TU in parallel, up to mono.
- Class lifecycle: Check/ClassLifecycle.k decides copyable / movable /
  trivial / destructible per record.
- Virtual destructor rule: a class deriving a base whose destructor is not
  virtual, when the derived class has a non-trivial destructor, is SC056E
  (also in ClassLifecycle.k). Without it, deleting a Derived through a Base
  runs only Base's destructor:

    class Base    { fn op delete (self) { ... } }
    class Derived derives Base { fn op delete (self) { ... } }

  The fix is `virtual fn op delete` on Base and `override` on Derived.
- Value categories (lvalue / xvalue / prvalue): assigned inline by
  ExprTyper, since overload resolution needs them while the call is still
  being typed; Check/ValueCategoryPass.k only verifies none is left Unknown.
- Mono: every use of a generic at concrete arguments registers an instance
  (AST/Context/InstantiationRegistry.k); the instance has one home TU that
  emits the specialization, and every other TU using it emits
  `extern template` (CODEGEN.md). Mono/Monomorphization.k checks lifecycle
  constraints (copy / move / non-transfer) at instantiation.

## Open

See RESOLUTION.md §4 and private/IMPORTANT/TICKETS.TXT. Notably: no
conformance check at a generic's instantiation site yet, and calls through
an interface bound that need an extension method wait on EmitWitness
(CODEGEN.md §7b).
